eights \u03a8(ti\u22121, ti) and Cij are calculated once for a given forward diffusion Eq. (1) and time discretization, and can be reused across batches. For some diffusion Eq. (1), \u03a8(ti\u22121, ti),Cij have closed form expression. Even if analytic formulas are not available, one can use high accuracy solver to obtain these coefficients. In DMs (e.g., VPSDE and VESDE), Eq. (15) are normally 1- dimensional or 2-dimensional integrations and are thus easy to evaluate numerically. This approach resembles the classical Adams\u2013Bashforth (Hochbruck & Ostermann, 2010) method, thus we term it tAB-DEIS. Here we use t to differentiate it from other DEIS algorithms we present later in Sec. 4 based on a time-scaled ODE.\nThe tAB-DEIS algorithm is summarized in Algo 1. Note that the deterministic DDIM is a special case of tAB-DEIS for VPSDE with r = 0. The polynomial approximation used in DEIS improves the sampling quality significantly when the number of sampling steps N is small, as shown in Fig. 4c."
